/-!
# Absolute geometry of `Spec ℤ`

The geometries in which `Spec ℤ` has a product with itself, tested against Weil's and Stepanov's
arguments for curves: the arithmetic square `Spec W(ℤ)` in ghost coordinates, its Frobenius graphs,
contacts and gluing, its universal property and `p`-derivations, its Teichmüller points and lattices
of sections, its archimedean fibre, the toric surfaces of a base, the carries, the comparison with
Weil's off-diagonal and with the adelic carrier, and Weil's archimedean form as the finite part of
the jets: the diagonal on combs of degree points, the symbol on the carrier, the form of every
packet on the degree points and on invariant combs, the whole pairing of the degree points as the
pole minus Weil's functional, and the tower of lines that carries the contacts with a count; the
pairing as twice the product of the degrees minus the sum over the zeros, its sign on bounded
apertures and its dependence on the contacts and on the comb; the ring of the tower, the pole
plane and the jets with its section count; the carry against the window count; the remainder of
the zero count on the square and its reading by the contacts at a height; the Hodge index of
the pairing; the carry as a cocycle class; the square of a Dedekind domain; the pairing of the
square of a quadratic ring with its conductor and its Hodge index; the square of a cyclotomic
ring with its contacts on a progression; the constants of the counterterm as the threshold
of the inequality; the planar square, a subring of the square with unit masses in which every
contact is paid; the squares that satisfy Bézout at an aperture as the window cone; the exact
Hodge index of the square of a number ring; Riemann–Hurwitz for the counterterm with the
genus of a degree point; the exchange law between the contacts and the constant of the
counterterm; the tree of a prime glued along the comb, whose edge length the inequality
forces; the length of the tree of `2` as a monotone deformation of the pairing on packets
shorter than `log 4`; the count of a section on two graphs, the split norm; the pole term
as the scale of the contacts; the zeros of a function of order one weighed by two vertical
lines, and Weil's explicit formula for every such function with a functional equation; Weil's form on step packets as the limit of the form on block-constant divisors, its
sign as the Riemann hypothesis, and the inequality at finite scale in both directions; the
path of a contact paid at its price, monotone at every prime; the Hecke `L`-functions of a
number field with their explicit formula, the tempered configuration of their zeros and their
Hodge index, the ideals of a number ring as degree points, and the inequality below the first
contact for a large discriminant; the count of sections in a box as the volume over the gluing
index; the form on the comb in the increments of the divisor; and the Li ladder as the forms
of the Laguerre tails themselves, with the form of every pair of logarithmic tails as a sum
over the zeros; and a ring that is not abelian, the ring of the cube roots of two, with its
degree points and its Hodge index, and the index law of its Artin character of degree two
through the cubic character of two; the matrix of Weil's form on step packets as the block
matrix of the degrees, the jets and the floor maps, with its margin on the first step packets of
ratio four and seven; the index law of the aperture path, the negative index at an aperture as
the sum of the nullities of the smaller apertures, whose total counts the zeros right of the
line; the index law of a virtual configuration, a
difference of zero configurations, which counts zeros and poles; the index as the number of
crossings of the conormals of the apertures with the graph of Weil's operator, the polar
Lagrangian of an aperture in the space of charges and forcings, whose crossings with the charge
axis are the charged null modes and which is an even line and an odd line, the index law in the
energy with the conservation law of a rectangle of apertures and energies, the simple ground
mode of a short aperture, and the polar Lagrangians below the ground energy as an increasing
path of positive forms; Weil's form on step packets as the product of the two charges, a
potential and the Dirichlet form of the jets and the floor maps, with a neutral part that is
Lorentzian on the first step packets; the margin of the block matrices of ratio at most
`103/50` at every block size; the neutral part as Weil's form of the zeros minus the poles; and
the Riemann hypothesis as one negative square of the neutral part of every window, with the
index law at finite scale: the block matrices carry the zeros right of the line and the neutral
part one square more, the pole; the sign of the block matrices of every ratio on thin step
packets; and the coarse blocks of a ratio, which carry the index at every block size and decide
the margin of every refinement; and the contacts of a number ring as its point counts over the
finite fields, with the relative square of two number rings at finite scale; the ground vector
of the block matrix of a narrow window, of one sign and charged; and the gluing of two forms
with one negative square each through the product of their charges; and the index of a power
of a quotient of `L`-functions, which is the index of the quotient; and a count of the bound
states of a window by the sinks of a positive trial vector, from its potential and its
couplings, with the closed form of the potential of a window without contacts and the count
one on the narrow windows; and the gluing of two adjacent windows, whose union holds at most
the bound states of the product model with potentials lowered by the excess of the couplings
over the product of the charges; and the forms with reweighted contacts, a tower under
refinement on which weakened or twisted contacts give a second bound state on the windows of
ratio three and four at every block size; and the count of bound states decided on the positive
cone: a second bound state is two positive packets without a common block whose coupling is
below the geometric mean of their depths, or a bound state that takes both signs; and the
two-sided potential brackets and response-matrix inertia law for multi-charge gluing, positive
potential and unbounded opposite-sign susceptibilities on narrow windows (`16J ≤ R`), and the
dichotomy at ratio four: the halves `(R, 2R]` and `(2R, 4R]` glue through rank-one charges iff
`R = 1`, while their raised model holds one bound state at every block size and rank-two charges
glue `(2, 4]` and `(4, 8]`. See
`AbsoluteGeometryOfSpecZ/README.md`.
